<div class="pt-6 md:pt-12">
  <p class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent mb-3">The thesis</p>
  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl mb-4">What I'm starting to think GTM Radar actually is.</h2>
  <p class="font-serif text-lg text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px]">
    GTM Radar today is a research artifact — four PDFs, a watchlist, a rubric I edit by hand. The version worth paying for is something else: a system that surfaces signals a recruiter can't see manually, ranked by accuracy that's been backtested across enough weeks to mean something.
  </p>

  <p class="font-serif text-lg text-ink leading-relaxed max-w-[640px] mt-4 font-semibold">
    And the real shift isn't faster intel on the names a recruiter already knows. It's surfacing the names she doesn't — three weeks before they know they're hiring, and before they show up on anyone else's radar.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-serif text-xl mt-12 mb-3">The question I keep coming back to</h3>
  <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px] mb-3 italic">
    What signal could the system see that a recruiter can't see manually?
  </p>
  <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px]">
    The honest answer isn't raw data. Crunchbase, Carta, LinkedIn alumni, BuiltWith — all of it is for sale. The edge is correlation. A recruiter reads one thing at a time. A system reads 55 companies across 8 signal types every week and learns which combinations predict which hire. That conditional probability, backtested over 18 months, is the thing that doesn't exist yet — and the thing worth building.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-serif text-xl mt-12 mb-4">Three categories I keep watching for</h3>

  <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px] mb-5">
    <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Org-shape signals.</span> A recruiter sees profiles. A system sees shape. Sales-to-engineering ratio shifting from 1:5 to 1:3 means a GTM scale-up is incoming. Marketing leadership flat for 24 months while the company 5x'd revenue means a CMO search within six. Tenure distribution skew — lots of sub-12-month hires alongside senior departures — means chaos, and chaos means active searches. You can compute shape from public LinkedIn alumni data and job postings. No recruiter is doing it.
  </p>

  <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px] mb-5">
    <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Departure-prediction signals.</span> The spicier one. Equity vest cliffs at Series B+ companies are predictable. Average VP Sales tenure at growth-stage SaaS is around 18 months — that's a base rate. LinkedIn activity patterns (skill additions, recommendation asks, profile photo changes) correlate to job-search behavior. Conference attendance shifts. Comp-review cycles producing Q2 and Q4 departures. Recruiters know individual people. A system knows the base rates, and can rank "most likely to be open" across 10,000 execs.
  </p>

  <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px]">
    <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Latency-edge signals.</span> Catch the event before LinkedIn does. SEC Form D filings hit weeks before press releases. Job posting deltas — VP Sales req deleted, CRO req added the next day — signal the announcement before it lands. Domain registrations, trademark filings, pricing-page changes (enterprise tier added → enterprise AE hires coming). The signal isn't "company X raised." It's "company X will announce raising in two weeks."
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-serif text-xl mt-14 mb-3">Why the briefings are the moat, not the product</h3>
  <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px] mb-3">
    Raw data is commoditized. The rubric — backtested over 18 months of weekly briefings, edited by a human who learned what fires and what fizzles — is not. Every edition is training data. Every hit and miss teaches the rubric. v1 isn't the product. v1 is the data collection apparatus for v2.
  </p>
  <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px]">
    The system's edge isn't seeing what recruiters can't see. It's correlating across what they could see if they had 200 hours a week. You're selling them their own time back, weighted by probability.
  </p>

  <h3 class="font-serif text-xl mt-14 mb-3">The honest caveat</h3>
  <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed max-w-[640px]">
    Most of these signals require real data infrastructure — paid APIs, vendor contracts, terms-of-service gray work. The trap for a non-technical builder is spending three months on plumbing and shipping zero briefings. The counter-move: vendor every signal you can, and spend the saved time on the rubric — the part nobody else can do because nobody else is reading 25 signals a week and editing them by hand.
  </p>
</div>
